  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Born in Batavia in the Dutch East Indies, del Prado endured Japanese wartime internment before moving with his family to the Netherlands. He studied chemistry and economics, spent time at Harvard Business School and encountered the young semiconductor industry in California. On returning home, he represented American technology suppliers before starting Advanced Semiconductor Materials in 1964.\n\nDel Prado turned ASM into a transcontinental equipment group, establishing operations in the United States, Japan and Hong Kong. He created ASM Pacific Technology, helped form ASML with Philips and pursued deposition technologies that became central to advanced chip production. The first Dutch company primarily listed on Nasdaq, ASM also became a seedbed for Europe’s equipment sector. Del Prado remained chief executive until 2008 and a committed long-term shareholder thereafter.",
    "html": "<p>Born in Batavia in the Dutch East Indies, del Prado endured Japanese wartime internment before moving with his family to the Netherlands. He studied chemistry and economics, spent time at Harvard Business School and encountered the young semiconductor industry in California. On returning home, he represented American technology suppliers before starting Advanced Semiconductor Materials in 1964.</p>\r\n<p>Del Prado turned ASM into a transcontinental equipment group, establishing operations in the United States, Japan and Hong Kong. He created ASM Pacific Technology, helped form ASML with Philips and pursued deposition technologies that became central to advanced chip production. The first Dutch company primarily listed on Nasdaq, ASM also became a seedbed for Europe’s equipment sector. Del Prado remained chief executive until 2008 and a committed long-term shareholder thereafter.</p>\r\n",
